course: english
lesson: 15
title: How to learn spellings that stick
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Freddie's mum says «just write it out ten times», and Freddie writes «becuase» ten times. He has now practised the mistake ten times. Practice only helps if it is accurate, which is why schools use look, say, cover, write, check: look at the word, say it, cover it, write it from memory, then check it letter by letter, and check again tomorrow.
explain: Memory likes little and often. Five words a night with a quick look back at last week's words beats twenty words crammed into one evening; this is called spaced practice. Lucas, who tries to learn twenty in one night, may pass on Friday and forget most of them by Monday.
explain: Use tools wisely. A dictionary gives spelling and meaning; a phone's spellcheck is quick but can offer the wrong word if your try is far off, so read its suggestion. If you can't find a word, think of other likely starts: «psychology» hides under ps, not s; «phone» under ph, not f; «knee» under kn, not n.
explain: Memory tricks, mnemonics, work for the hardest words («Big Elephants Can Always Understand Small Elephants» for because); rules work for most of the rest. Colour helps only if you colour the tricky bit, not the whole word in a rainbow. Writing your own sentence with each word fixes spelling and meaning together: «I accidentally dropped my sandwich.»
explain: Even keen readers can be weak spellers, because reading skims words without studying every letter; they need to pick a few words and look closely. Spelling still matters in school, jobs and any message people take seriously, even if an older cousin calls it something «for boomers». And practice fits anywhere: spell the signs, the menu and the car number plates on the way to school.
fact: Copying a word without checking can practise a mistake; practice must be accurate.
fact: Look, say, cover, write, check is a method for learning a spelling from memory.
fact: Spaced practice, small amounts over several days, works better than cramming in one night.
fact: A dictionary gives spelling and meaning; spellcheck is quick but may suggest the wrong word, so read the suggestion.
fact: Words with silent first letters are found under their real spelling: psychology under ps, knee under kn.
fact: A mnemonic is a memory trick; it helps with a few very hard words, and rules help with most others.
fact: Colouring helps only when it marks the tricky part of a word.
fact: Writing your own sentence with a word helps you remember its spelling and its meaning.
fact: Reading a lot helps, but many readers also need to study a few words closely to spell them.
fact: Spelling tests check progress; they are practice, not a judgement of the person.
fact: Spelling can be practised anywhere: signs, menus, number plates.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «boomer» is slang for older people (properly, people born in the years after the Second World War); «toque» is Canadian for a woolly hat; «eh» is a Canadian way to end a sentence; «my lover» is a friendly West Country greeting, and «proper job» there means well done.
exercise: Freddie writes «becuase» ten times. What went wrong? | He practised the mistake without checking | He wrote it too few times | He should have written it in pen | He wrote it too neatly
exercise: What are the five steps of the school method? | Look, say, cover, write, check | Read, copy, copy, copy, copy | Write, rub out, write, rub out, stop | Look, guess, ask, skip, forget
exercise: Ruby's brother says «just ask your phone». What is the risk? | Spellcheck may suggest the wrong word | Phones cannot spell any words at all | Phones make you forget how to read | There is no risk at all
exercise: A Year 5 pupil can't find «psychology» under s. Where should she look? | Under ps | Under sy | Under c | Under py
exercise: Gran in Bristol uses mnemonics. What is a mnemonic? | A memory trick | A spelling test | A kind of dictionary | A silent letter
exercise: Lucas learns 20 words in one night. What works better? | A few words a night, with old ones reviewed | 40 words in one night, twice as fast | One word a month, very slowly | No practice, just the test
exercise: Kiera's dad calls spelling tests a scam. What are they for? | Checking progress, as practice | Catching children out | Ranking families in the area by how clever they are | Nothing at all
exercise: Yasmin writes words in rainbow colours. How can colour really help? | Colour only the tricky part | Use more colours for longer words | Colour every letter differently | Colour the easy parts brightly
exercise: Sam's cousin says spelling is «for boomers». What does «boomer» mean? | An older person | A loud speaker | A baby | A rocket
exercise: Why write your own sentence with each word? | It fixes spelling and meaning together | It makes homework take longer, which teachers like | It helps handwriting only | Teachers like to read stories
exercise: Aaliyah reads lots of books but spells badly. Why can that happen? | Reading skims words without studying letters | Reading makes spelling worse | She reads the wrong books | Books use American spelling
exercise: What is a «toque» in Canada? | A woolly hat | A kind of car | A spelling book | A cup of coffee
exercise: Mum in Ottawa practises spellings in the car. Is that a good idea? | Yes, practice can happen anywhere | No, you need a quiet desk and silence | No, it distracts the driver every time | Only when the car is parked outside
exercise: What should you do with spellcheck's suggestion? | Read it and check it's the word you mean | Click it straight away, it is always right | Ignore it every time | Copy it ten times without looking
exercise: Where would you look for «knee» in a dictionary? | Under kn | Under n | Under ne | Under nn
exercise: Why is cramming 20 words in one night not ideal? | Much of it is forgotten after a few days | It is against school rules | Your hand would get too tired | Dictionaries close at night
test: «write it out ten times, that's how we learned». Freddie wrote «becuase» ten times. What's the fix? | Look, say, cover, write, check, and check it again tomorrow | Write it twenty times instead of ten | Write it in capital letters only | Stop practising and hope it sticks
test: «spellcheck'll sort it, spelling's for boomers lol». What's the problem? | Spellcheck can pick the wrong word if your try is far off | Spellcheck is always perfect, so there's no problem | Boomers invented spellcheck | Phones refuse to spell long words, so you need to know them yourself anyway
test: «grab ya toque, eh, spellings in the car». Which game helps? | Spell the signs and number plates you pass | Close your eyes and sleep | Sing loudly with no words | Count the cars only
test: What would happen if Lucas learned five words a night and reviewed old ones? | He'd remember more of them for longer | He would forget them all faster, because old words get in the way of new ones | He'd learn fewer words in total | Nothing different from cramming
test: «spelling tests are a scam, skip 'em, love». What could Kiera think instead? | The test is practice that shows what to work on | Her dad is right; tests are useless | Tests decide her whole future | Tests are only for teachers' fun, so she can skip them
test: «rainbow colours on every letter, so pretty, I'll remember». What's better? | Colour just the tricky bit | Use even more colours | Use glitter on every word | Write in black only
test: «she reads loads, so her spelling should be ace, innit». Why might it not be? | Readers skim; she should study a few words closely | Reading always makes spelling perfect | Books make spelling worse | She must be reading too fast to understand anything she reads
test: A cartoon kid looks up «fone» in the dictionary and gives up. What should he try? | Another likely start: ph, as in «phone» | Give up; it isn't in any dictionary | Look under the letter o | Ask the dictionary to find it for him, because books can hear you
test: «mnemonics for every word, proper job!» says Gran. Is that the best plan? | Use tricks for the hardest words and rules for the rest | Yes, a trick for every single word in the dictionary, even «cat» | No, tricks never help anyone | Only learn words with tricks
test: ★★★ Lucas learns 5 new words a night, Monday to Thursday, and on Friday reviews them all. How many words does he review on Friday? | 20 | 5 | 25 | 15
test: ★★★ Which word would you NOT find under its first sound in a dictionary? | gnome (sounds like n) | dog (sounds like d, under d) | sun (sounds like s, under s) | map (sounds like m, found under m)
test: ★★★ Freddie uses look, say, cover, write, check and writes «becuase» from memory. At which step should he catch it, and how? | Check: compare it letter by letter with the real word | Cover: the hidden word shows him the slip | Say: saying it aloud fixes the letters by itself | Look: he looks at his own wrong word again until it looks right to him
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of copying a word repeatedly without verification. | An erroneous spelling may thereby be practised and reinforced | The hand of the pupil is thereby rendered too fatigued | The word is thereby rendered too familiar to be remembered | No deficiency whatever attends the practice, which is the most efficient method known
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning spaced practice. | Small amounts studied over several days are retained better than amounts crammed into one session | Material crammed into a single evening is retained longest | Practice is of no benefit whatever to spelling | Practice is beneficial only when undertaken in silence at a desk, in the absence of any distraction whatsoever, for a period of not less than one hour
exam: Ascertain the most prudent use of an automatic spelling checker. | Its suggestions should be read and verified against the intended word | Its suggestions should be accepted without examination | It should be disregarded in all circumstances | It should be employed only for words of one syllable, longer words being beyond its competence
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a mnemonic? | A device to assist the memory, of particular use for exceptionally difficult words | A rule which governs the spelling of every word | A dictionary arranged according to sound rather than spelling, for the use of pupils | A test administered at the close of each week
